--TEST--
`queryAs<T>` answers a `Core\Db\Rows<T>` at the class its call site wrote, where `query` answers `Rows<Row>` — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 18: `Rows` is one generic class and not two. `query` fixes its `T` at
// a `Core\Db\Row`, and `queryAs<T>` at whatever the call site named — so the
// two members differ in their answer's *type argument* and in nothing else.
// That is the whole of what `PDO::FETCH_CLASS` was for, decided at the call
// site rather than by a mode flag set on the statement.
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::queryAs
use Core\Db\Derive;

#[Derive]
class Person {
    public uint $id;
    public tainted string $name;

    public function constructor(uint $id, tainted string $name)
    {
        $this->id = $id;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

// `rule:classes/no-traits` declares the member once, on `Core\Db\Queryable`, so a transaction
// answers exactly what its connection does — this function type-checks, and is
// the agreement half of the refusal below.
class People {
    public static function everyone(Core\Db\Transaction $tx): Core\Db\Rows<Person>
    {
        return $tx->queryAs<Person>("select id, name from people", []);
    }
}

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

Core\Db\Rows<Core\Db\Row> $wrong = $db->queryAs<Person>("select id, name from people", []);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Db\Rows<Core\Db\Row>`, found `Core\Db\Rows<Person>`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
